The distributive property helps us break down the multiplication into simpler steps, making it easier to verify the result.\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, the answer is correct as verified by the distributive property: 784.</answer>.<|User|>That's great! Can you also show me how to solve this using the area model method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>.\nCertainly! The area model method is a visual way to break down multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts. Here's how we can solve \\(16 \\times 49\\) using the area model method:\n\n1. **Break down 49 into 50 - 1:**\n   \\[\n   16 \\times 49 = 16 \\times (50 - 1)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Visualize the area model:**\n   - Draw a rectangle and divide it into two smaller rectangles.\n   - One rectangle will represent \\(16 \\times 50\\) and the other will represent \\(16 \\times 1\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:**\n   - The area of the first rectangle (representing \\(16 \\times 50\\)) is:\n     \\[\n     16 \\times 50 = 800\n     \\]\n   - The area of the second rectangle (representing \\(16 \\times 1\\)) is:\n     \\[\n     16 \\times 1 = 16\n     \\]\n\n4. **Add the areas of the two smaller rectangles:**\n   \\[\n   800 + 16 = 784\n   \\]\n\nSo, by using the area model method, we can see that:\n\\[\n16 \\times 49 = 800 + 16 = 784\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 16 and 49 is \\(\\boxed{784}\\).\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, the product of 16 and 49, using the area model method, is 784.</answer>",
